    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d172">

                <pb facs="0238" n="166" sameAs="frus:frus1909/pg166"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 5315/299.</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of
                            State to Chargé Fletcher.</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Telegram.—Paraphrase.]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1909-06-25"><hi rendition="#italic">June 25,
                        1909</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p>Mr. Wilson quotes telegram of the 22d instant from Berlin,<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d172-f1"/> and informs Mr. Fletcher that the terms on which American
                    bankers <pb facs="0239" n="167" xml:id="pg167"/> are willing to participate are
                    contained in department’s telegram of June 16. States for his own guidance, and
                    only if it should appear that there is some misunderstanding, that it should be
                    made clear that this Government, in waiving its just right for American capital
                    to cover one-half of the Hankow-Szechuen loan, expects substituted an American
                    share of one-fourth of the total sum involved in the whole of the present loan
                    agreement, including the Hupeh-Hunan section of the Canton-Hankow line, as well
                    as the Hupeh portion of the Hankow-Szechuen line. Refers to Mr. Fletcher’s
                    telegram of the 24th instant, and directs him to inform minister foreign affairs
                    that the Government of the United States is gratified to learn that the loan
                    will not be concluded without American participation. Adds that this Government,
                    however, insists upon its equal right to furnish materials.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
